// Lesson 3.3a — Fire Rate: Cooldowns & Time
// The rate-limiting problem: comparing timer functions in Unity (Time.time vs countdown timer vs Coroutines).
// Implementing the compound conditional cooldown in TurretController.cs.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.3a — Fire Rate: Cooldowns &amp; Time</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    In the previous lab, stepping in range triggered a continuous laser of 60 bullets every second.
    In software development, rate limiting is a fundamental problem: actions must not happen faster
    than a designated cadence. Today, you evaluate three different Unity timing functions and build
    a cooldown timer into <code>TurretController.cs</code>.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        How do we make an object "wait" between actions in Unity?
        Because <code>Update()</code> executes every frame without stopping, we cannot use a blocking
        sleep function (which would freeze the entire game window!).
        Instead, game engines offer three common patterns for timing:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Timing Approach</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>How It Works</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>1. <code>Time.time</code> Timestamp</strong></td>
            <td><code>if (Time.time &gt;= lastTime + cooldown)</code></td>
            <td>Records the absolute game time when an action occurred. Checks if the clock has advanced enough since the timestamp.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>2. Countdown Variable</strong></td>
            <td><code>timer -= Time.deltaTime;</code></td>
            <td>Decrements a float variable each frame. Fires when the counter reaches zero, then resets.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>3. Coroutines</strong></td>
            <td><code>yield return new WaitForSeconds(cooldown);</code></td>
            <td>Pauses a method execution asynchronously and resumes after a specified number of seconds without blocking the engine.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Evaluating Timing Approaches</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is the <code>Time.time</code> timestamp method particularly clean for single, independent actions like firing a shot or checking player damage?</li>
            <li>In what scenarios are <strong>Coroutines</strong> significantly more powerful than checking timestamps in <code>Update()</code>? (Hint: Imagine designing a boss attack that charges a laser for 1.5s, fires 5 rapid shots 0.2s apart, and then rests for 3s.)</li>
            <li>What happens if you use a local variable inside <code>Update()</code> for a timer instead of a class field? Why must timers live as member variables of the class?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Analyze the Architecture</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Examine how an industrial laser cutter uses compound boolean logic and timestamp tracking
        to prevent hardware overheating:
    </p>

    <pre><code>using UnityEngine;

// Industrial tool managing pulse intervals via timestamp gating
public class LaserDrill : MonoBehaviour
{
    // Designer knob: delay required between pulses
    [SerializeField] private float pulseCooldown = 1.5f;

    // Internal memory: timestamp of the previous discharge
    private float lastPulseTime = 0f;

    public void AttemptDischarge(bool sensorTargetAcquired)
    {
        // Compound condition: target is valid AND cooldown duration has elapsed
        if (sensorTargetAcquired &amp;&amp; Time.time &gt;= lastPulseTime + pulseCooldown)
        {
            FirePulse();

            // Reset the timestamp memory to current engine clock
            lastPulseTime = Time.time;
        }
    }

    private void FirePulse()
    {
        Debug.Log("Laser pulse discharged at: " + Time.time);
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If <code>lastPulseTime</code> is <code>4.0</code> and <code>pulseCooldown</code> is <code>1.5</code>, what is the earliest <code>Time.time</code> value at which <code>Time.time &gt;= lastPulseTime + pulseCooldown</code> evaluates to true?</li>
            <li>In boolean logic, if <code>sensorTargetAcquired</code> is <code>false</code>, does Unity bother evaluating the right-hand side of the <code>&amp;&amp;</code> operator? (This is called <strong>short-circuit evaluation</strong>).</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>TurretController.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Cooldown Tuning Knob:</strong> Declare a serialized private float field named <code>attackCooldown</code> with a default value of <code>2f</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Timestamp Memory:</strong> Declare a private float field named <code>lastAttackTime</code> initialized to <code>0f</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Compound Gating in Update:</strong> In <code>Update()</code>, modify your attack conditional to require <strong>both</strong> conditions using <code>&amp;&amp;</code>:
                <ul>
                    <li>The player must be within range: <code>distanceToPlayer &lt;= maxAttackRange</code>.</li>
                    <li>AND enough time must have passed: <code>Time.time &gt;= lastAttackTime + attackCooldown</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Reset Clock on Attack:</strong> Inside the <code>if</code> branch, call <code>AttackPlayer();</code>, and immediately record the current time by assigning <code>lastAttackTime = Time.time;</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save and return to Unity. Press Play and walk inside the turret's attack radius.
        Instead of a solid wall of bullets, the turret now fires a single projectile, pauses for 2 seconds,
        and then fires again!
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Timer Faults</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if you comment out the line updating <code>lastAttackTime</code>? Why does the turret revert back to firing 60 bullets per second?</li>
            <li>In the Inspector for Turret, change <code>Attack Cooldown</code> to <code>0.5</code>. Test it in play mode. How does the encounter pacing change?</li>
            <li>What happens if you initialize <code>private float lastAttackTime = 100f;</code> in your code? When the game starts, how many seconds must you wait before the turret can take its very first shot?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Testing Countdown Timers</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Instead of timestamp checking with <code>Time.time</code>, try engineering a countdown timer using <code>timer -= Time.deltaTime;</code>. When <code>timer &lt;= 0f</code>, fire and reset <code>timer = attackCooldown;</code>. Which timing approach feels cleaner to you?</li>
            <li>How could you use this exact same cooldown pattern to prevent the player from taking spike damage 60 times a second if they stand on a spike?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Compare three timing patterns in Unity (<code>Time.time</code>, countdown timers, Coroutines)</li>
            <li>Construct a compound boolean conditional using <code>&amp;&amp;</code></li>
            <li>Explain why the timestamp reset line (<code>lastAttackTime = Time.time</code>) is mandatory</li>
            <li>Tune a cooldown knob in the Inspector to balance game difficulty</li>
            <li>Commit your fire rate cooldown system to GitHub</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

